  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1143 AM MDT Wed Sep 2 2026\n\nShowers and storms continue to quickly exit the central\nmountains this afternoon, with a few other showers and storms\ntrying to form east of I-15 in the mountains. The latter area of storms\nshould remain isolated with coverage remaining under 15%. The\nbigger story is cooler temperatures and wind behind the cold\nfront. Gusts will continue to flirt with LAKE WIND ADVISORY\ncriteria on American Falls Reservoir and WIND ADVISORY from \nCraters through Monida Pass. For now, it looks like the\npotential is too low and not widespread enough to issue any\nheadlines. Some folks have likely already seen highs for the\nday, or will be back within a couple of degrees of where they\nwere ahead of the front. Afternoon temperatures in the central\nmountains should remain in the 50s and 60s, with 60s and 70s\nelsewhere. Parts of the Stanley Basin may struggle to get above\n50. Starting tonight and continuing through Saturday, any \npotential for showers and storms should remain mostly over the \ncentral mountains. We may see a few light showers and a few \nstorms during that period across the rest of eastern Idaho, but \nthis should be very limited in terms of time. Otherwise, it will\nremain breezy through Friday. Gusts will be lighter than today,\nbut still 25-35 mph at times...maybe pushing 40 mph in a few \nspots. Highs will slowly creep back up, but most of the time\nremaining in the 60s and 70s. A few low 80s may pop up on a\nthermometer across portions of the Snake Plain, Magic Valley,\nand valleys closer to the Utah border on Saturday. \n\nShowers and storms increase areawide Sunday and Monday. Low\npressure that will have been setting to our northwest swings\nfarther inland and to our northeast. This will draw moisture \nfrom the southwest, and try to tap into moisture from eventual \nHurricane Marie off the coast of Baja. The question is how much \nwill make it into Idaho. Trends this week have been 1)to keep \nthe low itself across the Pacific and not drawn into low \npressure across the western states, and 2) shunt most of the \nmoisture across Utah, Wyoming, and Colorado. There continues to \nbe fluctuations daily with the official forecast (WPC-based) for\nprecipitation. Compared to 24 hours ago, the forecast has \ndoubled (nearly tripled) for Sunday and Monday. The latest has \n0.25-0.50\" for the Snake Plain and Magic Valley, and 0.40-0.90\" \nfor higher elevations (highest across the eastern and southeast \nhighlands). The placement of higher amounts seems reasonable due\nto trending more south and east with the main push of moisture \nfrom Marie. The amounts seem overdone based on the trends we \nmentioned. When looking at the raw Blend of Models \"middle of \nthe road\" forecast, it is showing 0.10-0.33\" for the Snake Plain\nand Magic Valley and 0.25-0.75\" for the mountains with locally \nhigher amounts. This seems more reasonable and also what \nprevious WPC-based forecasts looked like. The higher end of the \nforecast range shows widespread 1\"+ amounts except for the Magic\nValley which is still 0.75-1.0\". Considering the trends, that \nwould definitely fall under \"low probability/high impact\". Even \nif we see less cloud cover and precipitation, we will stay \ncooler overall. Stay tuned.",
